\description{
Reads a Dynare model file and translates it into a nonlinear dsge
model, together with the calibration, shock standard deviations,
measurement errors, priors, optimal-policy problem and occasionally
binding constraints it declares, so that the model can be solved,
simulated or estimated in R without Dynare, MATLAB or Octave.
}
\details{
\strong{Declarations and model.} \code{var}, \code{varexo}, \code{varexo_det}, \code{parameters},
\code{predetermined_variables}, \code{varobs}, parameter assignments (and
constants assigned to undeclared names), the \code{model} block (including
\code{model(linear)}, equation tags and \verb{#} model-local variables), leads and
lags of any length on variables and shocks, and \code{STEADY_STATE(x)},
which always equals the steady state of \code{x} at the current parameter
values.

\strong{MATLAB code.} Dynare passes MATLAB statements in a \code{.mod} file
through to MATLAB; \code{read_dynare()} runs them with a built-in MATLAB
interpreter (matrices, cell arrays, structures, indexing, loops,
functions, \code{eval}, \code{fsolve}, \code{fzero}, \code{csolve}, ...), so calibrations
computed in MATLAB (e.g. \code{sigma = sqrt(V(1, 1))}, \code{verbatim} blocks or
\code{set_param_value()}) are reproduced. Parameters take the values they
have when the file first solves, simulates or estimates the model
(the first \code{stoch_simul}, \code{estimation}, ...); later assignments and
MATLAB code that post-processes results (plots, tables) are skipped and
listed in \code{notes}.

\strong{Macro processor.} \verb{@#define}, \verb{@#if}/\verb{@#elseif}/\verb{@#else}/\verb{@#endif},
\verb{@#ifdef}, \verb{@#ifndef}, \verb{@#for} (over arrays, ranges and tuples, with
optional \code{when} filters), \verb{@#include}, \verb{@#includepath}, \verb{@#echo},
\verb{@#error}, simple macro functions and \verb{@\{...\}} substitution are
expanded before the model is translated.

\strong{Timing.} No manual re-timing is needed. Every Dynare variable
becomes a control; lags become auxiliary state variables named
\code{x_lag1}, \code{x_lag2}, ...; leads beyond one period become auxiliary
controls \code{x_lead1}, ...; and each shock becomes an exogenous state that
holds the current innovation. Impulse responses therefore have the
same timing as in Dynare. The auxiliary variables also appear in
solution and IRF output.

\strong{Steady state and shocks.} \code{steady_state_model} becomes the model's
steady-state function (variables it does not set keep their \code{initval}
value, 0 by default, as in Dynare) and \code{initval} supplies starting
values for the numerical solver. A MATLAB steady-state file
\verb{<model>_steadystate.m} next to the \code{.mod} file is run with the MATLAB
interpreter instead, including helper functions in other \code{.m} files of
the same folder. Parameters that \code{steady_state_model} or the
steady-state file set (calibrated to targets) are recomputed from the
other parameters whenever the model is solved, as in Dynare. Models declared \code{model(linear)} are
linearised with an exact Jacobian. In the \code{shocks} block, standard deviations,
variances, covariances and correlations are supported; correlated shocks
are orthogonalised by Cholesky factorisation in \code{varexo} order, which
reproduces Dynare's impulse responses. Deterministic paths
(\code{periods}/\code{values}) are returned in \code{shock_paths}.

\strong{Observed variables and measurement errors.} A model may have fewer
observed variables than shocks. A \code{stderr} on an observed endogenous
variable (in \code{shocks} or \code{estimated_params}) is a measurement error: the
variable \code{y} is observed as \code{y_obs = y + y_me}, where \code{y_me} is an
i.i.d. shock. \code{\link[=estimate]{estimate()}} and \code{\link[=bayes_dsge]{bayes_dsge()}} rename a data column \code{y}
to \code{y_obs} automatically. With more observed variables than shocks and
measurement errors, the likelihood would be singular, so the extra
variables are dropped with a note.

\strong{Priors.} Dynare's mean/standard-deviation prior parameterisation is
converted to dsge's, exactly: \code{normal_pdf}, \code{beta_pdf}, \code{gamma_pdf},
\code{uniform_pdf}, \code{inv_gamma2_pdf}, and \code{inv_gamma_pdf} / \code{inv_gamma1_pdf}
(a prior on a standard deviation, translated to the \code{"inv_gamma1"}
distribution of \code{\link[=prior]{prior()}} with Dynare's own parameterisation). Shape
names are case-insensitive. Shifted or generalised priors and
\code{weibull_pdf} are not translated and are listed in \code{notes}.

\strong{Estimation options.} \code{presample}, \code{first_obs} and \code{nobs} from the
file's \code{estimation} command are stored in \code{estimation} and used by
\code{\link[=estimate]{estimate()}} and \code{\link[=bayes_dsge]{bayes_dsge()}}: the data are restricted to the
estimation sample and the first \code{presample} observations only
initialise the Kalman filter. The filter starts from the stationary
distribution (Dynare's \code{lik_init = 1}, the default) or, with
\code{lik_init = 2}, from Dynare's covariance of 10 times the identity on its
state vector (the observed and predetermined variables); other
\code{lik_init} values are reported in \code{notes}. Set \code{x$model$kalman_init}
to \code{NULL} to use the stationary initialisation instead.

\strong{Optimal policy.} With \code{planner_objective} and \code{ramsey_model} or
\code{ramsey_policy}, the planner's first-order conditions are derived
symbolically and added to the model together with Lagrange multipliers
\code{MULT_1}, ..., as in Dynare, so \code{\link[=solve_dsge]{solve_dsge()}} returns the Ramsey
equilibrium; the steady state is found with the multipliers concentrated
out. With \code{discretionary_policy} (linear models), the time-consistent
rule is computed with the Dennis (2007) algorithm at the calibrated
parameters and the model is closed with the planner's time-consistent
targeting rule. With \code{osr_params},
\code{osr_params_bounds} and \code{optim_weights}, \code{\link[=osr]{osr()}} can be called directly
on the imported model.

\strong{OccBin.} Equations tagged \code{bind = 'c'} / \code{relax = 'c'} and an
\code{occbin_constraints} block define occasionally binding constraints;
\code{\link[=simulate_occbin]{simulate_occbin()}} on the imported model solves them with the
piecewise-linear algorithm of Guerrieri and Iacoviello (2015), as
Dynare's \code{occbin_solver} does, using the file's \code{shocks(surprise)}
block by default.

\strong{Perfect foresight.} \code{initval}, \code{endval}, \code{histval}, \code{steady}, the
deterministic \code{shocks} (\code{periods}/\code{values}), \code{mcp} equation tags and
\code{perfect_foresight_setup(periods = )} / \code{simul} are stored in
\code{perfect_foresight}; \code{\link[=simulate_perfect_foresight]{simulate_perfect_foresight()}} solves the
deterministic path as Dynare's \code{perfect_foresight_solver} does.

\strong{MATLAB data and toolbox functions.} The MATLAB interpreter reads data
with \code{load} (Octave text files; MATLAB \code{.mat} files up to version 7 with
the \pkg{R.matlab} package), \code{xlsread} / \code{readmatrix} (spreadsheets, with
\pkg{readxl}), \code{csvread} and \code{dlmread}, and provides \code{fmincon},
\code{fminunc}, \code{lsqnonlin}, \code{hpfilter}, \code{ksdensity}, \code{interp1}, \code{polyfit}
and other common functions. When a file cannot be run (e.g. a data file
is missing), the error names the MATLAB statement that failed.

\strong{Not supported:} \code{external_function}, \code{trend_var}, \code{EXPECTATION()},
\code{diff()}, \code{adl()}, PAC and VAR expectation operators; these raise an
error. Other blocks and commands are recorded but not run, as is MATLAB
code that calls Dynare's internal functions (e.g.
\code{perfect_foresight_solver_core}).
}
\examples{
rbc <- read_dynare(system.file("examples", "rbc.mod", package = "dsge"))
rbc
sol <- solve_dsge(rbc)
irf(sol, periods = 20, se = FALSE)

# Model code can also be passed as text, including macro directives
ar <- read_dynare(text = "
  @#define lags = 2
  var y;
  varexo e;
  parameters rho;
  rho = 0.5;
  model;
    y = e
  @#for k in 1:lags
      + rho^@{k} * y(-@{k})
  @#endfor
    ;
  end;
  shocks;
    var e; stderr 0.01;
  end;
")
solve_dsge(ar)

}
